{
  "case_id": "2017-16-658",
  "document_hash": "66bbea8f13a0da897554019c326cd51d941d25143ec1557aa8aef5526186129a",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Jonathan A. Herstoff",
      "case_id": "2017-16-658",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "66bbea8f13a0da897554019c326cd51d941d25143ec1557aa8aef5526186129a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-66bbea8f13a0-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "One of the fundamental tenets of our constitutional structure is that only Congress can set the jurisdiction of the lower courts. Based on that fundamental principle, Federal Rule of Appellate Procedure 4(a)(5)(C) is non-jurisdictional. The plain language of 28 U.S.C. Section 2107(c) is unambiguous. A district court can extend the time to appeal as long as a motion is timely filed and there has been a showing of excusable neglect or good cause."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2017-16-658",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "66bbea8f13a0da897554019c326cd51d941d25143ec1557aa8aef5526186129a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-66bbea8f13a0-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Should we -- should we reach that question, counsel? I mean, the Seventh Circuit typed this limitation as jurisdictional, so they never went on to consider any question of forfeiture or waiver. So, are you suggesting that we should decide those questions in the first instance?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2017-16-658",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "66bbea8f13a0da897554019c326cd51d941d25143ec1557aa8aef5526186129a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-66bbea8f13a0-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: But -- but who misled her? Was it the court or was it her own attorney?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Professor Dodson",
      "case_id": "2017-16-658",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "66bbea8f13a0da897554019c326cd51d941d25143ec1557aa8aef5526186129a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-66bbea8f13a0-0000-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We have one brief, an amicus brief in this case, telling us that we were wrong in drawing a distinction between what's in a statute, jurisdictional, what's in the rule, non-jurisdictional. And instead, anything that shifts a case from one court to the other, as notice of appeal does, that should be considered jurisdictional. This is Professor Dodson's brief."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondents (Mr. Stewart)",
      "case_id": "2017-16-658",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "66bbea8f13a0da897554019c326cd51d941d25143ec1557aa8aef5526186129a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-66bbea8f13a0-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There's one fact that's undisputed in this case, and that is that Ms. Hamer filed her notice of appeal outside of the 30-day limitation that's set forth within Rule 4."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2017-16-658",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "66bbea8f13a0da897554019c326cd51d941d25143ec1557aa8aef5526186129a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-66bbea8f13a0-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But it was not raised in the district court, and that's the problem."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2017-16-658",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "66bbea8f13a0da897554019c326cd51d941d25143ec1557aa8aef5526186129a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-66bbea8f13a0-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Sorry, could you slow down and tell me why not? You were served with notice of the motion, weren't you?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondents (Mr. Stewart)",
      "case_id": "2017-16-658",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "66bbea8f13a0da897554019c326cd51d941d25143ec1557aa8aef5526186129a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-66bbea8f13a0-0001-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The very next day, the Seventh Circuit required the Respondents to brief the issue of jurisdiction. We responded seven or eight days later, notified the Seventh Circuit that, at"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2017-16-658",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "66bbea8f13a0da897554019c326cd51d941d25143ec1557aa8aef5526186129a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-66bbea8f13a0-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 32,
        "start": 32
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Stewart, do I understand you to no longer be relying on the argument that this is a jurisdictional rule?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2017-16-658",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "66bbea8f13a0da897554019c326cd51d941d25143ec1557aa8aef5526186129a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-66bbea8f13a0-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: How is that consistent with the adversary system? I mean, this isn't the other side -- the -- did she have an attorney at that point?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2017-16-658",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "66bbea8f13a0da897554019c326cd51d941d25143ec1557aa8aef5526186129a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-66bbea8f13a0-0002-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "No, she was pro se then at the time that the district court granted the motion for extension of time because the district court granted that along with the motion for withdrawal, so Ms. Hamer proceeded pro se from then on."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondents",
      "case_id": "2017-16-658",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "66bbea8f13a0da897554019c326cd51d941d25143ec1557aa8aef5526186129a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-66bbea8f13a0-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Respondents argued that -- that their -- their rights were not being enlarged because they got everything that they wanted from the district court summary judgment order."
    },
    {
